From c4a51d455cb3e09a61cde8022bd0fa7a38fcce43 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Tue, 8 Sep 2026 10:12:55 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] robo(reports): stop a long-held slab fabricating an extra
 day; fix first hour label
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Production Rate per Hour — three fixes from the report, all in the date/time
and hourly-bucketing logic (not by hiding labels):

1 & 3. Extra-day / cross-midnight. The hourly timeline reconstructs each
slab's day from the production SEQUENCE. Continuity was measured off the
previous slab's OUT time, so a slab held open for many hours (a long delay,
or a hold across a shift) finished with a late Out, and the next normal slab
then looked >12h "earlier" than the run so far — a FALSE midnight crossing
that drew a phantom day. That is what put a single-day batch into the next
date, and a genuine two-day batch (1386: 20 Jul 12:12 → 21 Jul 17:12) into a
third day (…22 Jul). Continuity is now carried IN-to-IN: In times move
forward across a real run, so the only >12h backstep they carry is a true
crossing (…23:55 → 00:04…), which still wraps exactly as before. A slab whose
own Out precedes its In is still treated as crossing midnight, so an overnight
slab's 00:09 Out is 21 Jul 00:09, never 20 Jul (issue 3). Preserves the
existing guarantees: an un-re-dated crossing is still reconstructed (CASE 2),
and forward-mis-dated slabs still do NOT open a day (BATCH 1432).

2. First X-axis label truncated ("18:00–19:00" showing as ":00–19:00"). The
pinned Y-axis is an opaque white copy held over the left edge while the plot
scrolls under it; it covered the full height, painting over the bottom-left
where the first angled time label's leading digits sit. Its white cover now
stops above the x-axis label band, and the first label gets a little more
left padding, so the whole range shows.

Tests: three new cases in roboHourlyProduction.test.ts — a single-day long
hold, the 1386 two-day long hold, and the issue-3 overnight Out — each
reproduces the bug (fails on the old Out-based continuity) and passes now;
all prior cases unchanged. No schema or DB change.

